# Text operations.
#
# The five primitives below are the ones §17.4.10 shows cannot be written
# in ZDeceptron, each for a reason that is a fact about the language rather
# than an inconvenience:
#
#   textLength, textAt  there is no way to inspect a Text from inside the
#                       language, so nothing can take one apart
#   uppercase,
#   lowercase           Unicode case mapping is a table, not a rule
#   trim                the lexer's string rule has no escapes, so no
#                       whitespace character can be written down at all
#
# `split` used to be the sixth, and the reason it no longer is says what
# was actually missing. It returns a collection whose length the source
# does not know, and §17.4.10 expected local bindings to close that. They
# did not: a binding gives a fold somewhere to keep its answer, which is
# what `sliceStep` below uses, and neither it nor `rest of` makes a list
# LONGER. `append item to list` does, and `split` is an ordinary fold
# above `textAt` now — the whole of the difference is that the fold has
# somewhere to put a piece when it finishes one.
#
# Everything under them is ordinary ZDeceptron, walking by index and
# carrying its answer with it.

foreign textLength is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "length"
    takes of value is Text
    gives Whole

foreign textAt is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "at"
    takes value is Text, index is Whole
    gives Option of Text

foreign uppercase is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "uppercase"
    takes of value is Text
    gives Text

foreign lowercase is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "lowercase"
    takes of value is Text
    gives Text

foreign trim is anywhere
    from "zd:text" as "trim"
    takes of value is Text
    gives Text

# The pieces of `value` between occurrences of `using`, in order.
#
# Written here rather than declared `foreign`, which §17.4.10 said was
# impossible: it hands back a list whose length the source does not know,
# and until `append item to list` existed nothing in the language could
# make a list longer. The separator is matched by `slice`, so it is
# compared a code point at a time and a multi-character separator needs no
# special case.
#
# `using is ""` is the characters of `value`, one piece each. JavaScript's
# own `split("")` divides a Text into UTF-16 units and hands back half a
# `🎉`; this divides it the way `textAt` indexes it, which is the same
# decision §5.4 makes everywhere else.
function split with value, using
    if using is ""
        give charactersFrom with value is value, index is 0, taken is empty
    give splitFrom with value is value, using is using, index is 0, piece is "", taken is empty

function charactersFrom with value, index, taken
    when textAt with value is value, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with ch
            give charactersFrom with value is value, index is index + 1, taken is (append ch to taken)

# The piece being gathered and the pieces already gathered both travel as
# parameters, so the last act is a call and the emitter turns it into a
# jump. A separator at the very end therefore yields a trailing empty
# piece, which is what the platform does and what the tests pin.
function splitFrom with value, using, index, piece, taken
    if index >= (textLength of value)
        give append piece to taken
    with stop is index + (textLength of using)
    if (slice with value is value, start is index, stop is stop) is using
        give splitFrom with value is value, using is using, index is stop, piece is "", taken is (append piece to taken)
    when textAt with value is value, index is index
        None
            give append piece to taken
        Some with ch
            give splitFrom with value is value, using is using, index is index + 1, piece is piece + ch, taken is taken

# The characters of `value` from `start` up to but not including `stop`.
#
# The characters gathered so far travel as a parameter rather than as a
# pending `ch + …` around the recursive call, so the last thing this does
# is call itself and the emitter turns that into a jump (§17.4.10). It is
# the difference between a `slice` whose stack depth is the length of what
# it slices and one whose depth is one frame.
function slice with value, start, stop
    give sliceStep with value is value, index is start, stop is stop, taken is ""

function sliceStep with value, index, stop, taken
    if index < stop
        when textAt with value is value, index is index
            None
                give taken
            Some with ch
                give sliceStep with value is value, index is index + 1, stop is stop, taken is taken + ch
    give taken

function startsWith with value, prefix
    give (slice with value is value, start is 0, stop is textLength of prefix) is prefix

function endsWith with value, suffix
    give (dropFirst with value is value, count is (textLength of value) - (textLength of suffix)) is suffix

function dropFirst with value, count
    give slice with value is value, start is count, stop is textLength of value

# The target of `value contains needle` when `value` is a Text.
function textContains with value, needle
    if needle is ""
        give yes
    give containsFrom with value is value, needle is needle, index is 0

function containsFrom with value, needle, index
    with stop is index + (textLength of needle)
    if stop > (textLength of value)
        give no
    if (slice with value is value, start is index, stop is stop) is needle
        give yes
    give containsFrom with value is value, needle is needle, index is index + 1

function isBlank of value
    give (trim of value) is ""
